abstract: >-
  A personal essay collection and structured knowledge graph mapping the three
  major Buddhist Abhidharma traditions (Theravāda, Sarvāstivāda, Yogācāra) onto
  contemporary frameworks in computational phenomenology — predictive processing,
  active inference, Global Workspace Theory, Parallel Distributed Processing, and
  related approaches. Comprises twenty-two essays (eleven themes × English + Japanese), a
  cross-tradition dharma comparison table (~130 dharmas across nine sheets), and
  a JSON-LD knowledge graph (353 nodes, schema.org + custom vocabulary) with
  bilingual labels and Sanskrit / Pāli / Chinese philological alignment. An
  interactive 2D/3D viewer is provided. v0.6.0 adds two themes. Theme 10 (Are
  the Four Noble Truths a tautology?) formalizes the Four Truths in Lean 4 over
  a space of models and sorts the inferential skeleton that holds in every
  model (granting the origin, cessation and the efficacy of a cause-removing
  practice follow by definition) from the content that is contingent and
  witnessed by countermodels (that there is dukkha, that the cause is craving,
  that a practice removing it exists). Theme 11 (Is sectarian dispute a failure
  of Buddhism?) applies W. B. Gallie's "essentially contested concepts" (1956)
  to Buddhist schism and to skillful means (upāya) in the Lotus Sūtra, sorting
  where the framework fits, where it clashes, and where readings split; it
  reads condition 7 (competition sustains the exemplar's achievement) as the
  one that fits worst, because texts handed down as the Buddha's word say
  disputation bears no fruit beyond praise and blame, and leaves open whether
  higher-order recognition loosens dispute or only refines grasping. Both are
  disclosed AI-mediated writing and state how far each source was checked.
  Developed in collaboration with the Claude
  language model (Anthropic) for synthesis, cross-tradition mapping, and graph
  encoding; the author retains responsibility for all claims.
